    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, my tomato plants are acting weird. the leaves on the bottom of the stems are turning this sickly yellow color but the top ones look okay. what's probably going on and how do i fix the soil?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The yellowing leaves on the lower stems of your tomato plants, a classic case of \"gollum's favorite leaf\" - the lower leaves are being drained of their nutrients, while the top ones are still getting the good stuff. It's a common issue, especially when the plant is under stress, like a hungry goblin under a rock, waiting for its next meal.\n\nThe likely culprit is a nutrient deficiency, perhaps a lack of magnesium or iron. You can try adding a balanced fertilizer to the soil, but first, let's get to the root of the problem (pun intended). Check the pH level of your soil - if it's too acidic or alkaline, it can cause nutrient deficiencies. A pH range of 6.0 to 6.8 is ideal for tomatoes.\n\nNow, about that soil... a good rule of thumb is to mix in some compost or well-rotted manure to improve the soil structure and fertility. You can also try adding some organic matter like peat moss or perlite to help retain moisture and prevent waterlogging. Just be careful not to overdo it, or you'll end up with a soil that's as soggy as a goblin's lair after a heavy rainstorm",
      "source": "target",
